  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Highlighted Rule",
    "overview": "KETGO looks like a calm, well-made study space. The page is light and quiet, deep teal does the work (the main button, the chosen option, the next-step panel), and one highlighter-lime mark shows the single sentence worth remembering, the way a learner marks a textbook. Everything else stays out of the way so the Rule can be read.\n\nThe look is commercial, sleek and clean, at the craft level of Apple, Revolut or Wise. It is never a generic tech startup (no gradients, glow, neon on dark) and never an official or government site (no grey, stiff, form-heavy screens). It grew out of the 2025 dark-teal-and-lime mocks but turned them light-first and took lime off buttons. Brutalist, experimental and metaphor-led looks were tried and rejected (2026-10-14).\n\nThe Question screen is the calmest place in the app: no motion, no decoration, one clear main button at the bottom. Landing and Home may carry a little more presence, through the night-teal panel and big tabular numbers, never through ornament.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Light first; dark follows the phone and keeps the same contrast.",
      "Deep teal for action, night teal for the \"next step\", lime only as a highlighter.",
      "One font (Atkinson Hyperlegible Next), two weights.",
      "Soft-rounded, flat surfaces with 1 px lines; one shadow in the whole system.",
      "Right and wrong always shown by icon + word + colour."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Highlighter Rule",
        "body": "Lime marks the one thing to remember, at most once per screen, plus the logo and the Memory Hook label. Never a button, never text colour, never a border.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Meaning Colours Rule",
        "body": "Green, red and amber mean right, wrong and trap. They are never used for decoration, and they never appear without an icon and a word.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 16 px Floor Rule",
        "body": "No sentence is smaller than 16 px. Label size (15 px) is for one- to four-word chips.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lithuanian Text Rule",
        "body": "Content is NFC-normalised at build time. Soft hyphens (U+00AD) are inserted at build time with `hyphen/lt` plus an exceptions list (for example `lygia·reikš·mių`, `dešinia·ran·kis`), with CSS `hyphens: manual`. Phones' own Lithuanian hyphenation is not relied on (it fails on iPhone and on Android 12 and older). Authors write plain text.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Font Budget Rule",
        "body": "One self-hosted variable WOFF2, weights 400–700, subset to Basic Latin, the 18 Lithuanian letters and the punctuation used (13,340 bytes). ✓ and ✕ are not in the font: draw them as SVG icons.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Shadow Rule",
        "body": "If a new element seems to need a shadow, give it a tone or a line instead.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** give every screen one Deep Teal main button, at the bottom when the screen is a task.",
      "**Do** show right and wrong with an icon, a word and a colour, every time.",
      "**Do** keep sentences at 16 px or larger and body text at 18 px with 1.5 line height.",
      "**Do** check every new colour pair: 7:1 for body text, 4.5:1 minimum, 3:1 for edges and icons.",
      "**Do** build the onboarding video from these real components as HTML/SVG animation with real-text captions and a still version for reduced motion.",
      "**Do** keep motion to 120 ms colour changes; respect reduced motion."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use lime on a button, as text colour, or more than once on a screen.",
